  "generated_text": "To determine the units digit of the sum \\(3^1 + 3^3 + 3^5 + 3^7 + \\ldots + 3^{2009}\\), we first need to identify the units digits of the powers of 3. We observe the pattern in the units digits of the first few powers of 3:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^1 & = 3 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 3)}, \\\\\n3^2 & = 9 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 9)}, \\\\\n3^3 & = 27 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 7)}, \\\\\n3^4 & = 81 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 1)}, \\\\\n3^5 & = 243 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 3)}, \\\\\n3^6 & = 729 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 9)}, \\\\\n3^7 & = 2187 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 7)}, \\\\\n3^8 & = 6561 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 1)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nWe see that the units digits repeat every 4 terms: 3, 9, 7, 1. Therefore, the units digits of the terms in the sum \\(3^1 + 3^3 + 3^5 + 3^7 + \\ldots + 3^{2009}\\) are the same as the units digits of \\(3^1, 3^3, 3^5, 3^7, \\ldots, 3^{2009}\\). This is an arithmetic sequence of exponents with a common difference of 2, starting from 1 and ending at 2009.\n\nTo find how many terms are in this sequence, we use the formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence:\n\\[\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d,\n\\]\nwhere \\(a_n = 2009\\), \\(a_1 = 1\\), and \\(d = 2\\). Solving for \\(n\\):\n\\[\n2009 = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 2 \\implies 2008 = (n-1) \\cdot 2 \\implies n-1 = 1004 \\implies n = 1005.\n\\]\nSo, there are 1005 terms in the sequence. We now need to find the units digit of the sum of these terms. Since the units digits repeat every 4 terms, we can group the terms into sets of 4:\n\\[\n(3^1 + 3^3 + 3^5 + 3^7) + (3^9 + 3^{11} + 3^{13} + 3^{15}) + \\ldots + (3^{2007} + 3^{2009}).\n\\]\nEach group of 4 terms has a sum of units digits:\n\\[\n3 + 7 + 1 + 9 = 20 \\quad \\text{(units digit is 0)}.\n\\]\nSince there are 1005 terms, there are \\(1005 / 4 = 251\\) full groups of 4 terms and 1 additional term. The sum of the units digits of the full groups is:\n\\[\n251 \\times 0 = 0.\n\\]\nThe units digit of the additional term \\(3^{2009}\\) is the same as the units digit of \\(3^1\\), which is 3. Therefore, the units digit of the entire sum is:\n\\[\n0 + 3 = 3.\n\\]\nThus, the units digit of \\(3^1 + 3^3 + 3^5 + 3^7 + \\ldots + 3^{2009}\\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
